      "explanation": "This question gets at the core, long-term legacy of the partition of Africa (Chapter 4.1). While 'effective occupation' accelerated the scramble, the most profound and lasting weakness of the entire Berlin process was the nature of the boundaries created. The European powers partitioned the continent based on their own rivalries and geographic conveniences, drawing straight lines on maps that cut across existing, organic communities. As a result, single ethnic groups (like the Ewe or Somali) were divided among two or three different colonies, and historical rivals were lumped together into a single administrative unit. When these artificial colonial territories later became independent nations, these imposed boundaries became a primary source of ethnic conflict, civil wars, and separatist movements. This disregard for the human geography of Africa is the most significant and tragic flaw of the partition, with consequences that persist to this day. The other issues are minor or procedural compared to this fundamental problem."
