      "answer": "The Oyo Empire, with its division of power between the Alaafin, the Oyo Mesi (council of chiefs), and the Ogboni society.",
      "explanation": "This is a sophisticated question that applies a modern political science concept ('consociationalism') to a pre-colonial African state (Chapter 2.4). Consociational democracy is a model for governing diverse societies where different major groups are all given a share of power, and no single group can dominate. It relies on mechanisms like grand coalitions, mutual vetoes, and proportional representation. The political structure of the Oyo Empire, while not a modern democracy, had strikingly similar features. Power was not concentrated in a single place. The Alaafin (the king) had executive and divine authority. The Oyo Mesi (a council of non-royal lineages) had the power to select and even depose the king (a mutual veto). The Ogboni (a society of notables) had judicial and religious power that acted as a further check on both the Alaafin and the Oyo Mesi. This deliberate fragmentation and sharing of power between different key institutions of the state can be interpreted as an indigenous form of consociationalism, designed to ensure stability by preventing any one segment from accumulating absolute power."
