      "explanation": "This question addresses a crucial ideological clash over African development in the 1980s (Chapter 10). The Lagos Plan and the Berg Report represent two completely opposite diagnoses and prescriptions for Africa's economic problems. The Lagos Plan was a product of the OAU and the UN Economic Commission for Africa. It was rooted in 'dependency theory,' arguing that Africa's problems were structural and caused by its subordinate position in a global capitalist system shaped by colonialism. Its solution was to break this dependency through regional integration and state-led industrialization (ISI). One year later, the World Bank issued the Berg Report ('Accelerated Development in Sub-Saharan Africa'). It completely rejected the dependency argument. It claimed Africa's problems were a result of its own bad internal policies: corrupt, inefficient state-owned enterprises, overvalued currencies, and too much government intervention. Its prescription was the exact opposite of the Lagos Plan: a full-scale adoption of free-market, neoliberal policies (which would become the basis for SAPs). This ideological collision defined the development debate for the next decade."
