"explanation": "This is a capstone question requiring a deep synthesis of the entire course. A 'palimpsest' is a manuscript page from which the text has been scraped or washed off so that the page can be reused, but on which traces of the original writing remain. As a historical metaphor, it means that the present is a layered reality where the past is never fully erased. The political map of modern Africa is not just the colonial boundaries (Layer 2). It is influenced by the memory and sometimes the surviving institutions of pre-colonial kingdoms (Layer 1), like the continuing influence of the Emir of Kano in Nigeria or the Asantehene in Ghana. It is also shaped by post-colonial developments like the creation of new federal states or the impact of civil wars (Layer 3). To understand contemporary Africa, one must be able to see all these layers at once—the ancient, the colonial, and the post-colonial—all coexisting and interacting on the same political 'surface'. This metaphor perfectly captures the complexity of African historical identity."
