"explanation": "This question requires linking the abolition of the slave trade (Ch 3.3) to the internal politics of a specific empire (Ch 2.4). The Oyo Empire's economy and military power in its later stages had become deeply enmeshed in the Atlantic slave trade. The state's elite derived significant wealth from trading captives, and this wealth was used to import horses and firearms, which in turn were used to maintain its powerful cavalry and conduct raids to acquire more captives. The British abolition of the trade created a severe crisis. It cut off a primary source of state revenue and the supply of modern weapons. Furthermore, it created a structural problem: what to do with the large, militarized class whose entire livelihood was based on slave raiding? This created massive social and political instability. Unemployed warriors and ambitious military chiefs (like in the rising city of Ibadan) turned their energies inward, contributing to the civil wars and power struggles that tore the empire apart from within throughout the 19th century. The end of the trade thus pulled the economic and military rug out from under the Oyo state."
