"explanation": "This is an advanced question on source criticism (Chapter 1.3 & 13.1). A good historian understands that reliability is not absolute; it depends on the question being asked. A ship's captain had a strong business reason to keep accurate quantitative records: how many 'units' of human cargo were loaded, how many died (for insurance/profit calculations), how many supplies were used. So, for numbers and dates, the logbook is likely a very reliable source. However, the captain had no reason (and every reason not) to record the human suffering. For that, the autobiography of a survivor is an unparalleled, essential, and more reliable source. It provides the qualitative data—the smells, the sounds, the fear, the despair—that the logbook completely omits. Both sources are biased, but a critical historian uses them in conjunction, leveraging the strengths of each to build a more complete picture of both the business and the brutal human reality of the slave trade."
